#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157068 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "e5b64c26-300d-530d-b22f-ff061f4e3296" :question/math-academy-id "e-7657" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{x} - 5 & > 0 \\\\ 4e^{x} & > 5 \\\\ e^{x} & > \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (e^{x}) & > \\ln (\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & > \\ln 5 - \\ln 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "95104c90-f452-5551-adec-836ef1192276" :question/math-academy-id "e-7660" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x < 7^{3-x}$." :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 5^{x} & < 7^{3 - x} \\\\ \\ln (3 \\cdot 5^{x}) & < \\ln (7^{3 - x}) \\\\ \\ln 3 + \\ln (5^{x}) & < \\ln (7^{3 - x}) \\\\ \\ln 3 + x\\ln 5 & < (3 - x)\\ln 7 \\\\ \\ln 3 + x\\ln 5 & < 3\\ln 7 - x\\ln 7 \\\\ x\\ln 5 + x\\ln 7 & < 3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3 \\\\ x(\\ln 5 + \\ln 7) & < 3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3 \\\\ x & < \\frac{3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3}{\\ln 5 + \\ln 7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/id #uuid "1c336514-aac7-5f9c-8118-8a16188f2d42" :question/math-academy-id "e-7703" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12$." :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} + 2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln [(\\frac{3}{4})^{x}] & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x\\ln (\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x(\\ln 3 - \\ln 4) & \\ge \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\nx \\mid \\le \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "185e4c00-f218-5d54-8345-397267a74b6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7704" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} + 12 & > 0 \\\\ 4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} & > - 12 \\\\ 3^{8 - x} & > - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0$, we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x$."}]}
